#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541143 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175978 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bff48a7c-66d6-55e8-99a1-ae8c80925335" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "089a813c-6c0e-5e0b-8e37-0be57131cd8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi \\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{-5}^{-2}y\\sqrt{2{y}^{2}+10y+13}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540541} {:answer/id #uuid "1a442bb6-9973-5336-9114-49d50247246c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi \\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{-5}^{-2}y\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+5y+1}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540570} {:answer/id #uuid "3b737ea3-db83-533e-bf9c-6d4d23c19b41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi \\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}(2y+5)\\sqrt{2{y}^{2}+10y+13}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540624} {:answer/id #uuid "42dcd5e7-1244-58d1-8b4c-96176ca2b293" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi \\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}({y}^{2}+5y+4)\\sqrt{2{y}^{2}+10y+13}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540641} {:answer/id #uuid "7b6b74f6-b844-5d38-9b76-3abb89103049" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi \\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}({y}^{2}+5y+4)\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+5y+1}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540735}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42dcd5e7-1244-58d1-8b4c-96176ca2b293" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi \\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}({y}^{2}+5y+4)\\sqrt{2{y}^{2}+10y+13}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540641} :answer-field/id #uuid "bfcd94c6-4a82-5d36-a7d1-d10e9ebad35a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541026}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd63787d-4bed-5aec-9b0b-525bc4b676f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-109259" :question/problem "![](images/d9/d9ad43f4bddaeab8327e5405193471c005962013355ec84c4a6f858640216dd7.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x=-{y}^{2}-5y-4$ that lies in the fourth quadrant between its $y$-intercepts. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{2}-5y-4 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}+5y+4 & =0 \\\\ (y+1)(y+4) & =0 \\\\ y & =-4,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=-4$ to $y=-1.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(-{y}^{2}-5y-4)=-2y-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-4}^{-1}(-{y}^{2}-5y-4)\\sqrt{1+{(-2y-5)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-2\\pi {\\int }_{-4}^{-1}({y}^{2}+5y+4)\\sqrt{26+20y+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-2\\pi \\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}({y}^{2}+5y+4)\\sqrt{2{y}^{2}+10y+13}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541085 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129318 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b02082a8-b56e-5478-859d-51db7273f765" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of X"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2aecc83e-62f9-5570-b3ec-23bbc0d4c666" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{2{e}^{t}+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540602} {:answer/id #uuid "434933b7-c938-5b99-9c27-958c4224b195" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540643} {:answer/id #uuid "602e3020-e08a-5741-9ce8-35913e0c5082" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{t/2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540692} {:answer/id #uuid "66c00b74-0490-5e7d-8d8b-1cd2cf6001b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540703} {:answer/id #uuid "cc74ab54-9cb6-55f6-a841-1af50094d0ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{2t}\\sqrt{{e}^{t/2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540867}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66c00b74-0490-5e7d-8d8b-1cd2cf6001b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540703} :answer-field/id #uuid "09078c01-f245-5447-88a1-e69ec53cbb38" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540946}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5b66c4d7-d9b1-5cb6-840d-18a9a3471ea0" :question/math-academy-id "q-138966" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={e}^{t},\\,y=2{e}^{t/2},\\,t\\in [0,4].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=4.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{t/2})={e}^{t/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}2{e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{{({e}^{t})}^{2}+{({e}^{t/2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}({e}^{t}+1)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{t/2}\\cdot {e}^{t/2}\\cdot \\sqrt{{e}^{t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541150 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101022 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55fab3ac-cad3-54bd-b07b-9159554b634c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Average Value of a Function Over a Given Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08723c80-e899-5f73-9042-d123c4781581" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{\\pi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540539}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08723c80-e899-5f73-9042-d123c4781581" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{\\pi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540539} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e0422d8-ab75-5b1f-88c6-2c6d1cf32970" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540957}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f3134f95-ccde-508a-8ade-bbcbdfc93226" :question/math-academy-id "q-344647" :question/problem "Find the average value ${g}_{\\text{avg}}$ of the function $g(x)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ on the interval $[-\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${g}_{\\text{avg}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\pi /4-(-\\pi /4))}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{2}{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)|}_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }[(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))-(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}))] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }[(2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-(2\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-4\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}))] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }[(-\\sqrt{2})-(\\sqrt{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\cdot (-2\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\sqrt{2}}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541098 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176020 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4728b3c6-a600-569d-922e-228e9f6fb2a6" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Carrying Capacity of a Logistic Growth Model In Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d330e3b-0a3b-5e96-a25c-b67e16d84426" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540715}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d330e3b-0a3b-5e96-a25c-b67e16d84426" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540715} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1a61518-8249-5d82-89b0-dd7a0d81fab9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541016}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "70edaacc-f97d-53a0-9bf0-62992d59dc46" :question/math-academy-id "q-353478" :question/problem "The number $y(t)$ of people who have adopted electric vehicles in a town follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4200y-7{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=55,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible number of people who will adopt electric vehicles?\n\nThe maximum possible number of people who will adopt electric vehicles is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible number of people who will adopt electric vehicles. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/d8/d8133f1c4203903bac606bbae61c73717e2ba2dc15a97a77f48eaaeaf4736d06.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4200y-7{y}^{2} \\\\  & =7y(600-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=600.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $600,$ and we conclude that the maximum possible number of people who will adopt electric vehicles is $600.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360827 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166887 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a03ad88-dc53-582c-b2ed-1aeb0ee2d0f5" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Binomial Probability Over an Unbounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1757289e-0855-595f-9588-9fbbdc1ac9b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0170" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357954} {:answer/id #uuid "48971816-b3a4-50b7-bba0-300278ce79b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0016" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358373} {:answer/id #uuid "7c82e5f7-f27d-5840-a6b8-3eb28d78e6ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0030" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358830} {:answer/id #uuid "8dcdf750-3b7b-5ae1-882c-ee9009f8df4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0084" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358987} {:answer/id #uuid "b9cf1c6a-e88c-5068-8968-1fd2e27e2e7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0154" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359363}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48971816-b3a4-50b7-bba0-300278ce79b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0016" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358373} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1c7f7e5-eeb2-5311-b80b-866939de58a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360308}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a2f49332-b965-5b6e-9513-00e213772e19" :question/math-academy-id "q-97510" :question/problem "Given that $X∼B(6,0.2),$ compute $P(X>4).$" :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(6,0.2),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x})(0.2)^{x}(0.8)^{6-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>4) & =P(X\\in \\{5,6\\}) \\\\  & =f(5)+f(6) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{5})(0.2)^{5}(0.8)^{6-5}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{6})(0.2)^{6}(0.8)^{6-6} \\\\  & =6(0.2)^{5}(0.8)+1(0.2)^{6}(0.8)^{0} \\\\  & =0.0016.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
